##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    1837 1451  221
                                                                  1837 1451  222
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       1837 1451  223
                                                                  1837 1451  224
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          1837 1451  225
                                                                  1837 1451  226
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   1837 1451  227
                                                                  1837 1451  228
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 1837 1451  229
                                                                  1837 1451  230
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     1837 1451  231
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   1837 1451  232
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             1837 1451  233
                                                                  1837 1451  234
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                1837 1451  235
                                                                  1837 1451  236
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       1837 1451  237
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  1837 1451  238
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   1837 1451  239
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  1837 1451  240
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  1837 1451  241
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 1837 1451  242
to the highest incident energy.                                   1837 1451  243
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:1837 1451  244
                                                                  1837 1451  245
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  1837 1451  246
                                                                  1837 1451  247
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    1837 1451  248
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     1837 1451  249
                                                                  1837 1451  250
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     1837 1451  251
                                                                  1837 1451  252
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    1837 1451  253
total cross section.                                              1837 1451  254
                                                                  1837 1451  255
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            1837 1451  256
                                                                  1837 1451  257
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    1837 1451  258
                                                                  1837 1451  259
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        1837 1451  260
                                                                  1837 1451  261
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  1837 1451  262
                                                                  1837 1451  263
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           1837 1451  264
                                                                  1837 1451  265
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   1837 1451  266
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        1837 1451  267
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     1837 1451  268
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         1837 1451  269
sections.                                                         1837 1451  270
                                                                  1837 1451  271
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 1837 1451  272
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 1837 1451  273
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 1837 1451  274
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 1837 1451  275
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      1837 1451  276
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       1837 1451  277
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              1837 1451  278
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  1837 1451  279
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  1837 1451  280
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  1837 1451  281
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  1837 1451  282
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  1837 1451  283
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1837 1451  284
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        1837 1451  285
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1837 1451  286
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        1837 1451  287
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1837 1451  288
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        1837 1451  289
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1837 1451  290
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        1837 1451  291
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1837 1451  292
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        1837 1451  293
                                                                  1837 1451  294
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           1837 1451  295
                                                                  1837 1451  296
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    1837 1451  297
discrete levels.                                                  1837 1451  298
                                                                  1837 1451  299
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         1837 1451  300
                                                                  1837 1451  301
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  1837 1451  302
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   1837 1451  303
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      1837 1451  304
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  1837 1451  305
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      1837 1451  306
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         1837 1451  307
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   1837 1451  308
on an angular grid.                                               1837 1451  309
                                                                  1837 1451  310
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       1837 1451  311
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1837 1451  312
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1837 1451  313
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1837 1451  314
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1837 1451  315
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1837 1451  316
                                                                  1837 1451  317
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     1837 1451  318
                                                                  1837 1451  319
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     1837 1451  320
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   1837 1451  321
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  1837 1451  322
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             1837 1451  323
All data are generated with TALYS.                                1837 1451  324
                                                                  1837 1451  325
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   1837 1451  326
                                                                  1837 1451  327
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      1837 1451  328
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   1837 1451  329
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   1837 1451  330
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are1837 1451  331
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   1837 1451  332
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  1837 1451  333
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              1837 1451  334
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 1837 1451  335
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  1837 1451  336
For each residual product:                                        1837 1451  337
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               1837 1451  338
- the recoil                                                      1837 1451  339
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         1837 1451  340
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     1837 1451  341
sections.                                                         1837 1451  342
                                                                  1837 1451  343
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     1837 1451  344
                                                                  1837 1451  345
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  1837 1451  346
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 1837 1451  347
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          1837 1451  348
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       1837 1451  349
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 1837 1451  350
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     1837 1451  351
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  1837 1451  352
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      1837 1451  353
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         1837 1451  354
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             1837 1451  355
                                                                  1837 1451  356
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   1837 1451  357
followed:                                                         1837 1451  358
                                                                  1837 1451  359
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1837 1451  360
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1837 1451  361
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1837 1451  362
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     1837 1451  363
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1837 1451  364
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1837 1451  365
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1837 1451  366
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1837 1451  367
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1837 1451  368
                                                                  1837 1451  369
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 1837 1451  370
                                                                  1837 1451  371
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    1837 1451  372
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          1837 1451  373
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     1837 1451  374
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          1837 1451  375
                                                                  1837 1451  376
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  1837 1451  377
                                                                  1837 1451  378
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     1837 1451  379
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1837 1451  380
as isomer.                                                        1837 1451  381
                                                                  1837 1451  382
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 1837 1451  383
                                                                  1837 1451  384
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        1837 1451  385
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1837 1451  386
as isomer.                                                        1837 1451  387
                                                                  1837 1451  388
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          1837 1451  389
                                                                  1837 1451  390
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:1837 1451  391
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   1837 1451  392
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   1837 1451  393
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   1837 1451  394
  given                                                           1837 1451  395
                                                                  1837 1451  396
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          1837 1451  397
                                                                  1837 1451  398
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  1837 1451  399
                                                                  1837 1451  400
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 1837 1451  401
                                                                  1837 1451  402
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         1837 1451  403
                                                                  1837 1451  404
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   1837 1451  405
                                                                  1837 1451  406
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        1837 1451  407
and more details are given above in this description. In most     1837 1451  408
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        1837 1451  409
                                                                  1837 1451  410
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 1837 1451  411
                                                                  1837 1451  412
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  1837 1451  413
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     1837 1451  414
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    1837 1451  415
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   1837 1451  416
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  1837 1451  417
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                1837 1451  418
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      1837 1451  419
   same reaction channel between different energies)              1837 1451  420
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      1837 1451  421
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               1837 1451  422
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      1837 1451  423
   different reaction channels for different energies)            1837 1451  424
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        1837 1451  425
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              1837 1451  426
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  1837 1451  427
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       1837 1451  428
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     1837 1451  429
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  1837 1451  430
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     1837 1451  431
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     1837 1451  432
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     1837 1451  433
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   1837 1451  434
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   1837 1451  435
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  1837 1451  436
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   1837 1451  437
                                                                  1837 1451  438
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  1837 1451  439
                                                                  1837 1451  440
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       1837 1451  441
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  1837 1451  442
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       1837 1451  443
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   1837 1451  444
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         1837 1451  445
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  1837 1451  446
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      1837 1451  447
representation is used.                                           1837 1451  448
                                                                  1837 1451  449
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      1837 1451  450
                                                                  1837 1451  451
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  1837 1451  452
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             1837 1451  453
                                                                  1837 1451  454
